\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}
\section{Introduction}
Visual SLAM systems accumulate redundant keyframes over long
trajectories, and map size grows linearly with operation time.
We propose an adaptive compression scheme that prunes keyframes
according to their marginal contribution to localisation accuracy,
so that the map stays bounded while place recognition keeps working.

\section{System Overview}
\begin{figure}
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{3cm}\rule{6cm}{0pt}}
\caption{Pipeline of the adaptive map-compression module.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}
Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} summarises the pipeline: incoming keyframes
are scored, clustered, and either retained or merged into an existing
map node.
The scorer runs on keyframe insertion, so compression adds no latency
to the tracking thread.

\section{Experiments}
We evaluate on four indoor sequences with repeated loop closures,
subsampling each trajectory to simulate weeks of operation.
The figure below reports the tracking time per frame as the
compression ratio increases.
\begin{figure}
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{3cm}\rule{6cm}{0pt}}
\end{figure}
Tracking time falls monotonically until the ratio reaches the point
where relocalisation failures begin to dominate the budget.

\section{Results}
A second figure breaks down the contribution of each module to the
final localisation error.
\begin{figure}
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{3cm}\rule{6cm}{0pt}}
\end{figure}
Removing the clustering stage hurts most, while the scoring heuristic
accounts for the remaining gap over the uncompressed map.

\section{Conclusion}
Adaptive compression keeps map growth bounded without measurable loss
of localisation accuracy on indoor sequences.
\end{document}

